# {{PROJECT_SLUG}}-shared

The Claude Code plugin every **{{PROJECT_NAME}}** repository enables, for the skills,
agents and hooks that are **specific to this project** and needed by its whole team.
It starts empty.

## What it is not for

The skills that read and maintain this knowledge base are the same in every project,
so they are not copied here. They ship in `tsh-product-management`, which every
repository of this project enables alongside this plugin, and they find the knowledge
base on their own:

| Skill | Does |
| :-- | :-- |
| `/tsh-product-management:navigating-project-context` | Reads the knowledge base before work that depends on project facts |
| `/tsh-product-management:writing-project-knowledge` | Adds or updates a document in the owning workspace, index and reviewer included |
| `/tsh-product-management:repairing-project-context-links` | Finds and repairs broken links, bad filenames and misrendering tables |
| `/tsh-product-management:adding-project-context-workspace` | Creates a workspace: folder, README, `CLAUDE.md` with an owner, area-map row |

A fix to one of them reaches every project with `/plugin update`. If one of them is
wrong for {{PROJECT_NAME}}, change it there or say so to its maintainers — do not fork
a copy into this plugin.

## The sharing rule

| A skill or agent is needed by… | It lives in |
| --- | --- |
| Every project the same way | `tsh-agentic-collections`, not here |
| More than one role or repository of {{PROJECT_NAME}} | This plugin |
| One code repository only | That repository's own `.claude/` |

Ties go to the code repository. Promoting later is cheap; a shared plugin full of
one-team tools taxes every session of every teammate.

## Enable it in a code repository

Run `/tsh-product-management:initializing-project-context` in the repository and choose
*Attach repository*. It asks whether the repository is one the team may commit config
to, then writes either a committed `.claude/settings.json` with this repository's git
URL — every teammate gets the plugin after accepting the trust dialog once — or, for a
client's repository, a `.claude/settings.local.json` that never leaves your machine and
that each teammate writes with the same command. The entry it writes:

```json
{
  "extraKnownMarketplaces": {
    "{{PROJECT_SLUG}}-context": {
      "source": { "source": "git", "url": "<this repository's URL>", "sparsePaths": [".claude-plugin", "plugins"] }
    },
    "tsh-agentic-collections": {
      "source": { "source": "github", "repo": "TheSoftwareHouse/agentic-collections" }
    }
  },
  "enabledPlugins": {
    "{{PROJECT_SLUG}}-shared@{{PROJECT_SLUG}}-context": true,
    "tsh-product-management@tsh-agentic-collections": true
  }
}
```

`sparsePaths` keeps the documents out of the plugin download; the knowledge skills read
them from your own clone of this repository.

## Adding a component

Add `skills/<name>/SKILL.md` or `agents/<name>.md` — **Markdown only**. No `hooks/`,
`.mcp.json`, `bin/` or scripts, no `Bash` in `allowed-tools` or `tools`, no `!` command
lines: everyone on the project installs this plugin, so anything executable belongs in
a company plugin, reviewed there. `scripts/check_plugin.py` enforces this in the
pipeline. Only `plugin.json` lives inside `.claude-plugin/`.

Name a new skill `{{PROJECT_SLUG}}-<what-it-does>`, so that with several projects
installed the `/` menu says which one it belongs to. Bump `version` in
`.claude-plugin/plugin.json` and add a [CHANGELOG.md](CHANGELOG.md) line in the same
pull request — without the bump the update reaches nobody, and the pipeline refuses it.
Teammates pick it up with `/plugin update {{PROJECT_SLUG}}-shared@{{PROJECT_SLUG}}-context`.
